{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Command Aaron and his sons, saying.</b> צַו means nothing other than exhortation — for immediate application and for the generations. Rabbi Shimon said: Scripture all the more needs to exhort in a matter where there is חֶסְרוֹן כִּיס (financial loss). There is exhortation only where there is laziness, as it is written ״עַצְלָה תַּפִּיל תַּרְדֵּמָה״ (Proverbs 19:15), and as it is written ״עַד מָתַי עָצֵל תִּשְׁכָּב״ (ibid. 6:9). And here He commands that he engage in the burning of fire all the night until the morning, and there is reason to fear that on account of the laziness found in man he may fall into a deep slumber and ruin the offering; therefore he requires exhortation. And a parallel to this the Ra'em explained regarding the matter of חֶסְרוֹן כִּיס: that there is great toil in the burning of the fire all the night, more than the pain of financial loss. And from here our Rabbis of blessed memory learned (Shabbat 20a) to say that priests in general are zealous, for Scripture exhorted them by saying ״צַו אֶת אַהֲרֹן וְאֶת בָּנָיו לֵאמֹר״ — for the generations: that they would all be zealous in the divine service and banish sleep from their eyes and slumber from their eyelids, and engage in the service all the night until the morning." "And regarding the matter of חֶסְרוֹן כִּיס mentioned here, opinions have multiplied, and I will add a fine teaching of my own. For the עֹלָה atones for the musings of the heart, and we find in the Jerusalem Talmudi (Terumot ch. 8, end of halakhah 4) an incident involving Rabbi Yohanan: he lost his purse, and they asked him a matter of Torah law and he did not know it. They said to him: Is it because your purse was lost that your mind was lost? He said to them: The mind depends on the heart, and the heart depends on the purse. From here is a proof that חֶסְרוֹן כִּיס will madden a sage and destroy the gift of understanding that Elohim has given him to contemplate in the orchard of the tree of life, and lead his heart to think and muse upon transgression. And if the priest did not offer this offering as required, he must make another, and the first is void and lost — thus the Hizkuni explained. And by means of this חֶסְרוֹן כִּיס his mind and heart will be lost and he will come to sinful thoughts — and then even the second offering will not atone for the musing, for it is a commandment that comes through transgression, and how can it atone for the musing when the offering actually increases his sinful musings on account of the financial loss? Therefore the priest requires exhortation all the more specifically in the עֹלָה offering." "Another matter: since this entire parashah is a command to the priests to say for the generations — to teach them ״זֹאת תּוֹרַת הָעֹלָה״ and ״תּוֹרַת הַחַטָּאת״, and likewise the entire Torah of the offerings — and teaching is presumably free of charge, as our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Nedarim 37a): Just as I [learned] for free, so you [must teach] for free. And moreover, the priests are presumably not wealthy, for they have no portion in the land, and their sustenance is day by day from the High Table. And on account of the חֶסְרוֹן כִּיס that is in them, we fear lest they lose their mind that depends on the purse. Therefore He came to exhort them and said ״צַו אֶת אַהֲרֹן וְאֶת בָּנָיו לֵאמֹר״ — that is, to say for the generations and to teach them ״זֹאת תּוֹרַת הָעֹלָה״, and likewise every ״זֹאת תּוֹרַת״ stated throughout the parashah. For it is all Torah of priests, and ״שִׂפְתֵי כֹהֵן יִשְׁמְרוּ דַעַת וְתוֹרָה יְבַקְשׁוּ מִפִּיהוּ״ (Malachi 2:7). Therefore He came to exhort them to strengthen their heart in the Torah of HaShem, to teach them for the generations, and that what happened to Rabbi Yohanan should not happen to them — that they were asked a matter of Torah and did not know — but rather as it says ״שְׁאַל נָא אֶת הַכֹּהֲנִים תּוֹרָה״ (Haggai 2:11) and so forth, and as Samuel said: the priests did not err (Pesahim 17b)." "<b>Aaron and his sons.</b> In the entire book of Leviticus, Aaron was not mentioned but rather 'the sons of Aaron,' and they said in the Midrash that the Holy One, blessed be He, distanced him on account of the sin of the calf, and Moses drew him near through his prayer and said: a hated well whose waters are beloved, etc., as is found in the Yalkut (Tzav, remez 599). And the reason for the matter is that all the offerings in the parashah of וַיִּקְרָא speak of the majority of those offerings that come on account of sin, and had they not sinned with the calf they would not have needed those offerings at all, as it is said ״כִּי לֹא דִבַּרְתִּי אֶת אֲבוֹתֵיכֶם בְּיוֹם הוֹצִיאִי אוֹתָם מֵאֶרֶץ מִצְרָיִם עַל דִּבְרֵי עוֹלָה וָזָבַח״ (Jeremiah 7:22). And the commentators said: specifically 'on the day I brought them out' — before they sinned with the calf — I did not command them concerning matters of burnt offering and sacrifice; but after they sinned with the calf I commanded them concerning the Tabernacle and offerings, for they then needed atonements. And since Aaron, who made the calf, was the cause of the offerings, therefore he was distanced from their performance — for a prosecutor cannot become a defender — until Moses drew him near through his prayer and said: to the trees You apportioned honor for the sake of their children, as is taught (Tamid 29a): 'All woods are fit for the arrangement except those of olive and grape,' etc. — and that is because wine and oil, their products, are offered upon the altar; therefore the trees, which are their fathers, were spared from being burned in the arrangement. So too Aaron would be spared for the sake of his children, for HaShem had said to destroy all his fruit, and the prayer of Moses availed for half, and on account of the half that was burned his sons' father emerged through death. And the Holy One, blessed be He, made known His ways to Moses, that so it would be — that the house would be sanctified through Aaron's sons — and upon this Moses founded the basis of this prayer." "<b>This is the Torah of the burnt-offering; it is the burnt-offering upon the burning-place.</b> ״הִוא הָעֹלָה״ is entirely superfluous, and the commentators agreed to say that it comes to inform us that whoever engages in the Torah of the burnt-offering is as if he offered a burnt-offering. And upon this it says ״זֹאת תּוֹרַת הָעֹלָה״ — whoever engages in the Torah of the burnt-offering, הִיא הָעֹלָה — it is the burnt-offering: this is as good as the offering of the burnt-offering itself. And we read it הִיא ('she'), while הוּא ('he') refers back to the scroll of the Torah, for סֵפֶר ('scroll') is masculine and תּוֹרָה ('Torah') is feminine; therefore it is written ״הוּא הָעֹלָה״ and we read it ״הִיא״." "And some say 'it is the burnt offering' — that the engagement in Torah is reckoned to a man as תּוּשִׁיָּה, for it drains a man's strength, as though he had offered himself as a burnt offering. And do not say that engagement in Torah is like bringing a burnt offering only by way of metaphor — for in truth there is here neither fire nor altar — Scripture comes to teach: 'עַל מוֹקְדָה עַל הַמִּזְבֵּחַ' (upon the fire, upon the altar), for the fire of the Torah that is within him is like the fire of the altar. And Scripture informs us that the time of the burnt offering is 'כָּל הַלַּיְלָה עַד הַבֹּקֶר' (all the night until the morning), and furthermore 'עַד הַבֹּקֶר' is superfluous, to teach the people a lesson by way of allusion: just as the time of the burnt offering is all the night until the morning, so the time suited for this — that one who engages in the Torah of the burnt offering is considered as though he offered a burnt offering — is the time of exile, which is likened to night, for then Israel must pay with the bulls of their lips. And 'עַד הַבֹּקֶר' — and not at all inclusive, for at the time when the morning of Israel dawns, then they will offer bulls upon the altar of HaShem in very truth, as it is said (Psalm 51:17) 'ה׳ שְׂפָתַי' etc., and when do I seek that You accept the utterance of my lips? 'כִּי לֹא תַחְפֹּץ זֶבַח וְאֶתֵּנָה' (for You do not desire sacrifice, or I would give it) (Psalm 51:18). And the language of 'כִּי' indicates time, meaning: for there will be a time when You will not desire sacrifice, and for that time I seek that You accept the utterance of my lips in place of the offering — for through engagement in the Torah of the offerings a person also arrives at a broken spirit. This is what Scripture says (ibid. 19): 'זִבְחֵי אֱלֹהִים רוּחַ נִשְׁבָּרָה לֵב נִשְׁבָּר וְנִדְכֶּה אֱלֹהִים לֹא תִבְזֶה' (The sacrifices of Elohim are a broken spirit; a broken and contrite heart, Elohim, You will not despise). And from His saying 'לֹא תִּבְזֶה' (You will not despise), infer from this that engagement in the offering itself is more choice to HaShem than engagement in the Torah of the burnt offering. And what He said, 'זֹאת תּוֹרַת הָעֹלָה הִוא הָעֹלָה' (this is the Torah of the burnt offering, it is the burnt offering) — that is, specifically 'כָּל הַלַּיְלָה' (all the night): in exile, when there is no sacrifice or meal-offering. And this is what it says afterward (ibid. 20–21): 'הֵיטִיבָה בִרְצוֹנְךָ אֶת צִיּוֹן' (Do good in Your favor to Zion) etc., 'אָז תַּחְפֹּץ זִבְחֵי צֶדֶק אָז יַעֲלוּ עַל מִזְבַּחֲךָ פָרִים' (then You will desire sacrifices of righteousness; then they will offer bulls upon Your altar)." "And it may be that on this account it is said (Jeremiah 7:21): 'עוֹלוֹתֵיכֶם סְפוּ עַל זִבְחֵיכֶם וְאִכְלוּ בָשָׂר' (Add your burnt offerings to your sacrifices and eat flesh) — that the prophet rebukes them for coming to the house of HaShem with burnt offerings and peace offerings, whose flesh is eaten by the owners: and do not bring about the destruction of the Temple and the cessation of the offerings, and do not lean on a crushed reed of love, saying that we are able to exempt ourselves from the offerings with words of burnt offering and sacrifice — that is, the speech of our lips. 'כִּי לֹא דִבַּרְתִּי אֶת אֲבוֹתֵיכֶם וְלֹא צִוִּיתִים בְּיוֹם הוֹצִיאִי אוֹתָם מֵאֶרֶץ מִצְרַיִם עַל דִּבְרֵי עוֹלָה וָזָבַח' (For I did not speak to your fathers nor command them on the day I brought them out of the land of Egypt concerning matters of burnt offering and sacrifice) (ibid. 7:22) — specifically concerning the matters of burnt offering and sacrifice I did not command on the day of redemption, that is, on the day I brought them out of the land of Egypt — for that is the morning of Israel. And the verse that says 'זֹאת תּוֹרַת הָעֹלָה הִוא הָעֹלָה' is specifically 'כָּל הַלַּיְלָה עַד הַבֹּקֶר' — and not at all inclusive, for the morning is the time for an actual sacrifice. And He said: 'וְאֵשׁ הַמִּזְבֵּחַ תּוּקַד בּוֹ' (and the fire of the altar shall burn in him). If He speaks of fire in general, it means: it shall burn in him — in the person who brings the offering — for fire kindles within him out of being bewildered and terrified, recoiling and saying: What is this I have done, for I have made my deeds evil and squandered my life, for I was deserving of all those punishments. And so the fire of the altar works upon him and moves him to return in repentance. And if He speaks of the fire of the Torah's law, then plainly it burns in him literally, consuming within him every evil thought and all the bestiality that is in him." "And by way of the allusion that He mentioned — the darkness of exile, which is likened to night — at that very time 'מִמָּרוֹם שָׁלַח ה׳ אֵשׁ בְּיַעֲקֹב' (from on high HaShem sent fire into Jacob), and one may fear lest, heaven forbid, it consume from soul to flesh. And where is the morning of which He spoke? Therefore He said that the fire from above burns but does not consume, for 'אֵשׁ הַמִּזְבֵּחַ תּוּקַד בּוֹ' — in Israel; for all fire from above, such is its nature: it burns and does not consume. And the fire of the altar proves it. And as our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Hagigah 27a): the transgressors of Israel — the fire of Gehinnom has no sway over them; all the more so from the golden altar, upon which there was only the thickness of a gold dinar and it was not consumed — all the more so these, who are full of mitzvot like a pomegranate. And the Tanḥuma (Exodus 15) concludes that fire from above has this nature: it burns and does not consume, and a proof from the fire of the bush — for the bush was not consumed. And this is a wondrous lesson upon what is written 'מִמָּרוֹם שָׁלַח ה׳ אֵשׁ' — to tell you that His fires are unlike His arrows, for His arrows consume but Israel are not consumed; so too His fires which HaShem sent are from on high and will not blaze to consume them. Rather, a fire like the fire of the altar shall burn in him until the morning — until the morning of Israel dawns." "And what we said — 'כָּל הַלַּיְלָה עַד הַבֹּקֶר' alluding to the time of exile — this interpretation is compelled by the midrash that the Yalkut concludes: 'זֹאת תּוֹרַת הָעֹלָה' — Rabbi Abba bar Yudan said: a parable of a king whose friend honored him with a cask of wine and a basket of figs. The king said to him: This is a gift?! He replied: My lord the king, for the time being I have honored you, but when you enter the palace, you will know with what I honor you. So the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Moses: 'זֹאת תּוֹרַת הָעֹלָה הִוא הָעֹלָה' — in astonishment. Moses said before Him: Master of the Universe, for the time being I have offered it, but when You do good in Your favor to Zion and build the walls of Jerusalem, then You will desire sacrifices of righteousness — a burnt offering, wholly consumed." "And a difficulty arises on this midrash: where is this reply alluded to in the verse, for the question is here and the answer is in the book of Psalms (51:20–21)? Rather, it is certain that the author of this midrash relied on the superfluous 'עַד הַבֹּקֶר' stated here, and interpreted that Moses answered the Holy One, blessed be He: specifically 'כָּל הַלַּיְלָה' — in this world, which is likened to night — You accept this; and 'עַד הַבֹּקֶר' — to the time of redemption, when the morning of Israel dawns, that is, 'when You do good in Your favor to Zion' — and not at all inclusive, for then You will desire sacrifices of righteousness. And he seized, along the way, the language of the verse in Psalms, but he derived this entire reply from the superfluous 'עַד הַבֹּקֶר.' And the intent of the saying is that the burnt offering of this world comes on account of the sin of thought — and is this honor and a gift? For there is no honor to HaShem in a person sinning and bringing an offering, for it would have been better had he not sinned and not brought an offering, for this offering is no gift at all but the payment for the sin. And does one honor the king with payment of his debt?! Therefore He brought a parable of figs and wine, because it is said (Hosea 9:10): 'כַּעֲנָבִים בַּמִּדְבָּר מָצָאתִי יִשְׂרָאֵל כְּבִכּוּרָה בִתְאֵנָה' (Like grapes in the wilderness I found Israel, like the first fruit of the fig tree in its first season). For in the wilderness Israel sinned, with evil ones among them and alongside them, as will be explained, with God's help, on the verse (Numbers 11:4) 'וְהָאסַפְסֻף אֲשֶׁר בְּקִרְבּוֹ הִתְאַוּוּ תַּאֲוָה' — see there. And with this He likened them to grapes, which appear beautiful from without but within are dregs and seeds; and figs have a stem on the outside, as the Yalkut concludes in parashat Lekh Lekha (section 16) regarding the matter of circumcision: just as the fig has no dregs but only its stem, etc. And the wine comes after the removal of the dregs within, and the basket of figs comes after they have cut off the stems. And He likened to them the offerings that atone for the sin of deed and thought, from within and from without. And the reply comes on this: 'כָּל הַלַּיְלָה' — in this world, while a person is entangled in the darkness of his matter, then 'there is no righteous man on earth who does good and does not sin' — specifically as long as he is on earth, joined to the earthly, material part within him. But when the morning of redemption dawns, then the heart of stone will be removed from Israel's midst, then You will desire sacrifices of righteousness — a burnt offering wholly consumed: not a burnt offering for sin, but peace offerings and a freewill burnt offering that come by way of righteousness and gift. Then bulls that come by way of righteousness and gift will be offered for acceptance. And this is a precious interpretation." "<b>A continual burnt-offering upon the altar, etc.</b> Three mentions of kindling are recalled in the parashah. And Mahari explained that they correspond to the three prayers, for the prayers were established corresponding to the daily offerings: the morning prayer corresponding to the morning daily offering, and the afternoon prayer corresponding to the daily offering of between the evenings, and the evening prayer corresponding to the limbs and the fat that were consumed all night long. And this is what it says: ״עַל מוֹקְדָה עַל הַמִּזְבֵּחַ כָּל הַלַּיְלָה עַד הַבֹּקֶר״ — corresponding to the evening prayer, whose time is all night long until morning; and concerning this it says ״וְאֵשׁ הַמִּזְבֵּחַ תּוּקַד בּוֹ״. And afterward it says ״וְהָאֵשׁ עַל הַמִּזְבֵּחַ תּוּקַד בּוֹ וּבִעֵר עָלֶיהָ הַכֹּהֵן עֵצִים בַּבֹּקֶר בַּבֹּקֶר״ — corresponding to the morning prayer. And afterward it says ״אֵשׁ תָּמִיד תּוּקַד עַל הַמִּזְבֵּחַ לֹא תִכְבֶּה. וְזֹאת תּוֹרַת הַמִּנְחָה״ — this corresponds to the afternoon prayer; up to here is the gist of his words. And a kind of proof for his words, that prayer is hinted at in the language of kindling, is that it says (Psalms 39:4) ״בְּהָגִיגִי תִּבְעַר אֵשׁ דִּבַּרְתִּי בִּלְשׁוֹנִי״." "And I am adding to his words, to bring solid and good proofs for this. For at first it says ״וְאֵשׁ הַמִּזְבֵּחַ תּוּקַד בּוֹ״ — there is no prohibition of a negative commandment here, but only a positive commandment. And in the second it says both a negative and a positive, as it says: ״וְהָאֵשׁ עַל הַמִּזְבֵּחַ תּוּקַד בּוֹ לֹא תִכְבֶּה״ — תּוּקַד בּוֹ is a positive commandment, לֹא תִכְבֶּה is a negative commandment. And in the third, both negative and positive are also said, and to it was added the matter of constancy, as it says: ״אֵשׁ תָּמִיד תּוּקַד עַל הַמִּזְבֵּחַ לֹא תִכְבֶּה״. And all this was not written incidentally but with great and wondrous intention — for the evening prayer is optional and is not an obligation, therefore no negative commandment is stated concerning it; and even the positive is not a complete positive, for there is no command directed at the fire, since it says ״וְאֵשׁ הַמִּזְבֵּחַ תּוּקַד בּוֹ״. And the morning prayer is an obligation and it is forbidden to nullify it; therefore it added the negative of ״לֹא תִכְבֶּה״. And for the afternoon prayer, beyond the negative of ״לֹא תִכְבֶּה״ it said ״אֵשׁ תָּמִיד״, which indicates constancy — that one should not nullify it on account of any business in the world, as is concluded in tractate Berakhot (6a): Rabbi Ḥelbo said: A person should always be careful with the afternoon prayer, for Elijah was answered only in the afternoon prayer, etc. Rabbi Yoḥanan says: Also in the evening prayer, as it says (Psalms 141:2) ״תִּכּוֹן תְּפִלָּתִי קְטֹרֶת לְפָנֶיךָ מַשְׂאַת כַּפַּי מִנְחַת עֶרֶב״. Rav Naḥman bar Yitzḥak said: Also in the morning prayer, as it says (Psalms 5:4) ״ה׳ בֹּקֶר תִּשְׁמַע קוֹלִי בֹּקֶר אֶעֱרָךְ לְךָ וַאֲצַפֶּה״. You see that the language of תָּמִיד and the language of לְעוֹלָם indicate zealousness and constancy in the afternoon prayer; therefore it says here ״אֵשׁ תָּמִיד״, and there they say: לְעוֹלָם יִזָּהֵר אָדָם, etc." "But this statement still needs clarification, for these three opinions were not cited in the order of their rank — for he should have fixed the words of Rabbi Yoḥanan last, so as to arrange them in the order of 'not only this but even that': not only the afternoon prayer but also the morning prayer, and not only the morning prayer but even the evening prayer. Therefore it seems to me that all three of them are speaking about the afternoon prayer alone, for each of them extends the time for the afternoon prayer, as is concluded in Tur Oraḥ Ḥayim siman 208 concerning one who nullifies a given prayer — whether it has a make-up after two prayers have passed, for according to Ba'al Ha-lakhot Gedolot there is a make-up even if two prayers have passed, and according to Rabbeinu Yonah even if its day has passed, as is concluded in the Beit Yosef. Therefore these three Amoraim saw fit to speak of the excellence and praise of the afternoon prayer: Rabbi Ḥelbo says: A person should always be careful with the afternoon prayer, in the manner that it says ״אֵשׁ תָּמִיד״ — that he should not nullify it for any business in the world." "Rabbi Yoḥanan adds to extend the time for the afternoon prayer: even if one was prevented by circumstances beyond his control and did not pray the afternoon prayer, none the less he should be careful to make up the prayer in the evening immediately following it. And this is what he said: אַף בִּתְפִלַּת עַרְבִית — that is to say, he should be careful to pray the afternoon prayer even in the evening, as it says: ״מַשְׂאַת כַּפַּי מִנְחַת עָרֶב״. And it is difficult: why did he call the evening prayer מִנְחָה? But surely he is speaking of one who forgot to pray the afternoon prayer and prays the evening prayer twice — first the evening prayer, and the second as a make-up for the afternoon prayer. Therefore it is said of the evening prayer ״תִּכּוֹן תְּפִלָּתִי קְטֹרֶת לְפָנֶיךָ״ — this is the evening prayer that comes at the time of the offering of incense between the evenings, that is, at the time of the kindling of the lamps; and afterward he prays one prayer for the afternoon prayer — that which he called ״מִנְחַת עָרֶב״." "And Rav Naḥman added to say further: אַף בְּשַׁחֲרִית — that is to say, even in the morning there is a make-up for the afternoon prayer, even though two prayers have passed and the day itself has passed, as it says: ״בֹּקֶר תִּשְׁמַע קוֹלִי בֹּקֶר אֶעֱרָךְ לְךָ וַאֲצַפֶּה״. And it is difficult: בֹּקֶר בֹּקֶר — why do I need both? For one cannot say that he is speaking of one who forgot the evening prayer and prays the morning prayer twice, since the evening prayer is optional, and something that is optional does not require a make-up. Moreover, since it specifically says ״אֶעֱרָךְ לְךָ״, surely this morning is speaking of the afternoon prayer, concerning which it says (Leviticus 6:7) ״הַקְרֵב אוֹתָהּ בְּנֵי אַהֲרֹן לִפְנֵי ה׳״ — something that is not said of any other one of them; and ״לִפְנֵי ה׳״ stated here is the same as ״לְךָ״ stated there, for this prayer is closer to HaShem more than all of them. From here one learns to say that this verse is speaking of one who forgot the afternoon prayer and did not pray it even in the evening, and prays in the morning twice — the first is the morning prayer, concerning which he said: ״ה׳ בֹּקֶר תִּשְׁמַע קוֹלִי״; and the second for the make-up of the afternoon prayer, and concerning it he said: ״בֹּקֶר אֶעֱרָךְ לְךָ וַאֲצַפֶּה״ — that he would wait and look forward until he first prays the obligatory morning prayer. And he said ״לְךָ״ in the pattern of ״לִפְנֵי ה׳״ stated here, for the afternoon prayer is closest of all to HaShem, the attribute of mercy — for at night the attribute of judgment prevails, therefore the evening prayer is not so much a time of favor, and consequently it is optional. And the morning prayer is somewhat close to the night that has passed. But the afternoon prayer, which is at midday, is removed from both these boundaries, as it is written (Psalms 37:6): ״וּמִשְׁפָּטְךָ כַּצָּהֳרָיִם״. Therefore Scripture arranged them in this parashah in the order of their rank: evening prayer, morning prayer, afternoon prayer." "And so did David arrange them, saying (Psalms 55:18): ״עֶרֶב וָבֹקֶר וְצָהֳרַיִם אָשִׂיחָה וְאֶהֱמֶה״ — corresponding to the numerical value of ״וְאֶהֱמֶה״ which is fifty-seven blessings in the three prayers together with the blessing of the heretics. And according to this, what is meant is: a person must always be scrupulous about the afternoon prayer above all three. And so it is written ״אֵשׁ תָּמִיד תּוּקַד עַל הַמִּזְבֵּחַ״. And concerning the matter of constancy he said ״וְזֹאת תּוֹרַת הַמִּנְחָה״, for its law too is forever, and one must always be scrupulous in it. And it is also consistent with what is written ״וּבִעֵר עָלֶיהָ הַכֹּהֵן עֵצִים בַּבֹּקֶר בַּבֹּקֶר״, in the manner of what is written: ״HaShem בֹּקֶר תִּשְׁמַע קוֹלִי, בֹּקֶר אֶעֱרָךְ לְךָ״ — and that is to provide a second morning for the afternoon prayer, as stated." "<b>All night until the morning.</b> The commentators said: because the burnt-offering comes to atone for the stirring of the heart, whose principal time is at night, as it is written (Micah 2:1) ״חוֹשְׁבֵי אָוֶן וּפוֹעֲלֵי רָע עַל מִשְׁכְּבוֹתָם בְּאוֹר הַבֹּקֶר יַעֲשׂוּהָ״; therefore its time is all night. But in the Tanḥuma (Pinḥas 13, see there) we find a contradiction to this opinion, for it concludes there: ״צֶדֶק יָלִין בָּהּ״ (Isaiah 1:21) — Rabbi Yehudah bar Simon said: no person ever lodged in Jerusalem with sin in his hand. How so? The morning tamid would atone for the transgressions of the night, and that of between the evenings would atone for the transgressions of the entire day. This is what is written: ״צֶדֶק יָלִין בָּהּ״, and it is written (Isaiah 33:24): ״הָעָם הַיּוֹשֵׁב בָּהּ נְשׂוּא עָוֹן״. For ״יָלִין״ speaks of lodging in it, so it was necessary to bring the verse ״הָעָם הַיּוֹשֵׁב בָּהּ״ for the daytime, since that is a time of sitting and not a time of lodging and lying down. And according to this, the tamid of the night would atone for the transgressions of the day. And there is a further difficulty: why did he say that the time of the tamid of between the evenings is all night, and why did he not mention this regarding the morning tamid, whose time is all day? And there is a further difficulty: in parashat Tetzaveh and in parashat Pinḥas he did not mention the morning burnt-offering except once, whereas the tamid of between the evenings he mentioned twice. And it appears that since the sin of the day is twofold — in deed and in thought — and moreover all day long a person is free to transgress, therefore he mentioned twice for between the evenings, for its atonement is twofold, and its time is all night to atone for the sin of the entire day. But the night is not a time of action, and for the most part the principal sin of the night is in thought, as it is written ״חוֹשְׁבֵי אָוֶן וּפוֹעֲלֵי רָע עַל מִשְׁכְּבוֹתָם בְּאוֹר הַבֹּקֶר יַעֲשׂוּהָ״, and moreover most of the night the person sleeps on his bed. If so, the morning burnt-offering atones only for the thought, and he did not specify its time as all day, for it does not need to atone for the sin of the entire night."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :register {:flags {9 ["God"]}}, :notes {7 "The phrase 'קַל וָחֹמֶר מִמִּזְבַּח הַזָּהָב' reproduces the Gemara's a fortiori argument in Hagigah 27a and is left in its literal form.", 1 "The verse cited at the end of halakhah 4 in y. Terumot 8 appears there in connection with a lost purse; the Kli Yakar seems to recall the location as 'end of halakhah 4' — retained as he wrote it.", 15 "The reference to ברכת המינים ('blessing of the heretics') is the twelfth blessing of the Amidah; 'fifty-seven blessings' likely counts the numeric total of the three prayer services' blessings plus this one.", 6 "In the verse from Jeremiah, the Kli Yakar reads עַל דִּבְרֵי עוֹלָה וָזָבַח as 'concerning words of olah and sacrifice' (i.e., lip-service), interpreting דִּבְרֵי through דִבּוּר 'speech'; this double sense is preserved by rendering 'matters of burnt offering and sacrifice.'", 3 "The final sentence 'his sons' father emerged through death' (יָצָא אֲבִיהֶם בְּדִימוֹס) is obscure; read hyper-literally. 'בְּדִימוֹס' may be a corrupted or transliterated Greek loanword, here rendered 'through death' without confidence.", 12 "The citation 'Tur Oraḥ Ḥayim siman 108' follows the Hebrew text as given, though the reference to siman 108 for laws of make-up prayers is retained as the author wrote it.", 5 "The word תּוּשִׁיָּה (a Rabbinic term for wisdom/Torah) is linked by the Kli Yakar to the root מָתַשׁ 'to drain/melt away,' which is a folk etymology; I preserved the implicit wordplay by rendering 'drains a man's strength.'", 16 "The phrase 'the tamid of the night' appears paradoxical since no night tamid offering exists; it is rendered literally as it stands.", 10 "The Hebrew text of the verse phrase has been normalized from the source's slightly variant spelling to standard form."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:37:29.355337301Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}